    "problem_statement": "URL path forced absolute when netloc is empty\n\n#### Description\n\nWhen creating a furl object with an empty netloc but a path, the path is incorrectly being forced to absolute even when it should remain relative.\n\n#### Steps/Code to Reproduce\n\n```python\nimport furl\n\n# This should create a relative URL but path becomes absolute\nf = furl.furl('//')\nprint(f.url)  # Expected: '//' but getting something else\n\n# Another case - empty netloc with path\nf = furl.furl()\nf.path = 'test'\nprint(f.url)  # Path behavior changes unexpectedly\n```\n\nThe issue appears when working with URLs that have empty netlocs but contain paths. The path composition logic seems to be forcing paths to absolute when they shouldn't be."
  }
